00:36:03.890 --> 00:36:05.760
Is really into like,

00:36:05.760 --> 00:36:07.810
I'm gonna make a bunch of
cocktails for everybody.

00:36:07.810 --> 00:36:10.820
So there's just that,
that just doesn't exist.

00:36:10.820 --> 00:36:14.230
But I mean, there's fun to be had

00:36:14.230 --> 00:36:17.160
with a nice cocktail or three,

00:36:17.160 --> 00:36:19.670
depending on how you're
spacing it and who's around.

00:36:19.670 --> 00:36:22.713
I mean, if you can't have fun
like that with your family,

00:36:23.820 --> 00:36:25.220
then who are you gonna have it with?

00:36:25.220 --> 00:36:27.545
You're gonna have it
at your brother's house

00:36:27.545 --> 00:36:28.643
and you're can break your coccyx.

00:36:29.630 --> 00:36:32.380
Yeah. You just got to
know your environment.

00:36:32.380 --> 00:36:34.250
I just don't want my kids to grow up

00:36:34.250 --> 00:36:36.399
and then them to be totally comfortable

00:36:36.399 --> 00:36:39.420
drinking responsibly
and then they come home

00:36:39.420 --> 00:36:41.770
and visit me and they feel
like they got to tiptoe around.

00:36:41.770 --> 00:36:44.530
That's a strange thing that
is not going to happen,

00:36:44.530 --> 00:36:48.860
but it was something that
happened with my family.

00:36:48.860 --> 00:36:50.085
I get that. Yeah.

00:36:50.085 --> 00:36:53.440
Oh, we're drinking a beer, but
it's out here by the grill.

00:36:53.440 --> 00:36:54.903
We're not going to drink one by the table.

00:36:54.903 --> 00:36:55.736
I don't think there's any danger of that

00:36:55.736 --> 00:36:57.400
being the case in your family.

00:36:57.400 --> 00:36:58.233
Moving forward.

00:36:58.233 --> 00:36:59.066
Yeah. Right.

00:36:59.066 --> 00:37:01.900
But it's an interesting
dynamic still going back home

00:37:01.900 --> 00:37:04.750
for Christmas. Like if we went
back next year, it's like,

00:37:06.320 --> 00:37:09.240
I bet we would have had
a little bit more fun

00:37:09.240 --> 00:37:13.230
with a little bit of, you
know, social lubrication.

00:37:13.230 --> 00:37:14.210
And some people just need that just

00:37:14.210 --> 00:37:17.550
to get through being with their families.

00:37:17.550 --> 00:37:21.780
Yeah, I mean, let's not talk about it

00:37:21.780 --> 00:37:23.670
as a coping mechanism.

00:37:23.670 --> 00:37:26.308
I'm just saying, for some people...

00:37:26.308 --> 00:37:27.141
Well, we could.

00:37:27.141 --> 00:37:28.670
Listen, I get it.

00:37:28.670 --> 00:37:30.370
Okay. Here we go.

00:37:30.370 --> 00:37:33.440
Give me another embarrassing
story. Don't hold back.

00:37:33.440 --> 00:37:36.373
This one is from Lena.

00:37:37.240 --> 00:37:38.530
She got the name Chia Lincoln.

00:37:38.530 --> 00:37:40.163
I'm lit right now, by the way.

00:37:41.210 --> 00:37:42.530
I'm just joking.

00:37:42.530 --> 00:37:43.363
On water?

00:37:44.483 --> 00:37:46.333
chialincoln on Twitter. Wow. Okay.

00:37:48.037 --> 00:37:50.970
"We always go for a Christmas Eve meal

00:37:50.970 --> 00:37:52.290
at my local restaurant.

00:37:52.290 --> 00:37:55.000
It's like a buffet self-serve
thing for my village."

00:37:55.000 --> 00:37:56.237
Good luck with that.

00:37:56.237 --> 00:37:58.080
"So, one year when I was 13,

00:37:58.080 --> 00:38:00.270
I was walking back to my family's table

00:38:00.270 --> 00:38:02.560
and tripped on a cable
running across the floor

00:38:02.560 --> 00:38:05.277
to power the Christmas
tree lights and fell over."

00:38:06.130 --> 00:38:08.497
Moving to the second tweet.
This is a long story.

00:38:08.497 --> 00:38:12.560
"Smashing my plate and
food went everywhere.

00:38:12.560 --> 00:38:15.850
They cut the Christmas music
to see what was going on."

00:38:15.850 --> 00:38:17.477
Was there a record scratch sound effect?

00:38:17.477 --> 00:38:20.950
"I was so embarrassed.
I just laid motionless,

00:38:20.950 --> 00:38:24.510
pretending I'd passed out
because that seemed like

00:38:24.510 --> 00:38:27.040
the least embarrassing thing to do.

00:38:27.040 --> 00:38:29.800
My parents still tease me
about it every Christmas."

00:38:29.800 --> 00:38:32.320
Lena played possum. They cut the music

00:38:32.320 --> 00:38:34.280
and they're looking around
to see what happened

00:38:34.280 --> 00:38:38.210
to the lights, and she's
laying face down in the dirt.

00:38:38.210 --> 00:38:40.271
Just still.

00:38:40.271 --> 00:38:42.330
This is remarkable.

00:38:42.330 --> 00:38:44.940
At a certain point, you got to get up.

00:38:44.940 --> 00:38:46.900
Yeah. But slowly as if you passed out.

00:38:46.900 --> 00:38:48.220
I mean, why does that make it better?

00:38:48.220 --> 00:38:49.830
It just makes people more concerned.

00:38:49.830 --> 00:38:53.663
Yeah. But it does make it
significantly less embarrassing.

00:38:53.663 --> 00:38:54.890
I mean, I got to say-

00:38:54.890 --> 00:38:57.900
Oh, you're with this?
Playing possum situation?

00:38:57.900 --> 00:39:00.240
Tripping, like, okay.

00:39:00.240 --> 00:39:04.670
Do you want people to like
be mocking you with applause?

00:39:04.670 --> 00:39:06.770
Like they do at restaurants
when things fall,

00:39:06.770 --> 00:39:08.630
or do you want people to be
coming up and being like,

00:39:08.630 --> 00:39:10.540
Oh my goodness, are you okay?

00:39:10.540 --> 00:39:11.690
We thought you had died.

00:39:11.690 --> 00:39:15.050
Now, let me just say,
I would never do this

00:39:15.050 --> 00:39:19.710
because I am the King of
trying to play things off,

00:39:19.710 --> 00:39:21.860
even if they can't be played off.

00:39:21.860 --> 00:39:24.370
So I would try to act
like it was no big deal

00:39:24.370 --> 00:39:29.370
and maybe try to like, say
something, but I like this plan.

00:39:29.780 --> 00:39:32.740
Just face down, quiet
until someone comes up

00:39:32.740 --> 00:39:34.040
and rolls you over.

00:39:34.040 --> 00:39:38.670
I mean, just the complex nature

00:39:38.670 --> 00:39:41.860
of the emotion of embarrassment.

00:39:41.860 --> 00:39:44.600
Can we just have an aside
about that for a second?

00:39:44.600 --> 00:39:48.680
You know, you have to know so much

00:39:48.680 --> 00:39:52.690
about how your personality
and your culture

00:39:52.690 --> 00:39:54.860
and your situation is constructed

00:39:54.860 --> 00:39:56.230
so that you you've done something

00:39:56.230 --> 00:40:00.270
to screw it up and you feel intense shame.

00:40:00.270 --> 00:40:02.440
Is that what it is? It's just like,

00:40:02.440 --> 00:40:05.640
I have done something that, you know,

00:40:05.640 --> 00:40:07.850
most of the time you feel
embarrassed for things

00:40:07.850 --> 00:40:10.810
that it could have been anybody, you know,

00:40:10.810 --> 00:40:15.276
everybody's tripped and fallen and like,

00:40:15.276 --> 00:40:18.400
stopped the lights, or, you know,

00:40:18.400 --> 00:40:19.880
there's been ramifications.

00:40:19.880 --> 00:40:22.140
But when it happens to you,

00:40:22.140 --> 00:40:25.350
it's as if you're the only
person that's ever happened to.

00:40:25.350 --> 00:40:28.720
And it's usually something
you, like you said,

00:40:28.720 --> 00:40:30.150
you didn't intend to do.

00:40:30.150 --> 00:40:30.987
It's not the spirit of the holidays.

00:40:30.987 --> 00:40:33.350
You didn't intend it to go this way.

00:40:33.350 --> 00:40:36.010
I think what you're getting at
is it would be very difficult

00:40:36.010 --> 00:40:38.450
to make a robot embarrassed.

00:40:38.450 --> 00:40:43.160
Like think of the code that
would have to go into an AI

00:40:44.800 --> 00:40:46.740
in order to program embarrassment.

00:40:46.740 --> 00:40:50.990
And do we want embarrassment
to be a part of the code?

00:40:50.990 --> 00:40:53.030
I mean, I think you do, right?

00:40:53.030 --> 00:40:56.210
It's hard to trust someone
who can't be embarrassed.

00:40:56.210 --> 00:41:00.930
It certainly is admirable
when somebody like just owns

00:41:00.930 --> 00:41:04.640
something that normally
would be embarrassing.

00:41:04.640 --> 00:41:08.590
Like you stand up and be like, I tripped.

00:41:08.590 --> 00:41:11.460
I also didn't think we
needed lights this year.

00:41:11.460 --> 00:41:12.293
Or, you know.

00:41:12.293 --> 00:41:13.900
Saying something to play it off, for sure.

00:41:13.900 --> 00:41:15.950
But I've never thought about it this way,

00:41:20.025 --> 00:41:21.550
but wouldn't you find it difficult

00:41:21.550 --> 00:41:23.730
to trust someone who
couldn't be embarrassed?

00:41:23.730 --> 00:41:25.853
Someone who was literally shameless.

00:41:26.800 --> 00:41:27.633
Wouldn't that be? I mean,

00:41:27.633 --> 00:41:31.600
either they were so well adjusted and so-

00:41:31.600 --> 00:41:34.530
Shameless implies like
doing things on purpose

00:41:34.530 --> 00:41:36.900
that most people would be
embarrassed to do by accident.

00:41:36.900 --> 00:41:40.230
Well, they didn't have
the ability to have shame

00:41:40.230 --> 00:41:43.300
or just didn't have the
ability to be embarrassed,

00:41:43.300 --> 00:41:45.780
regardless of what they
said and what they did.

00:41:45.780 --> 00:41:47.630
I mean, there are people like that.

00:41:50.170 --> 00:41:54.400
My aunt Fey, she was like that.

00:41:54.400 --> 00:41:55.940
I think she's passed away.

00:41:55.940 --> 00:41:56.773
Fey passed away.

00:41:56.773 --> 00:41:58.750
I'm embarrassed to say that, like,

00:41:58.750 --> 00:42:01.580
we've lost so many people
on that side of the family

00:42:01.580 --> 00:42:04.900
in rapid succession that like,
I think she was one of them,

00:42:04.900 --> 00:42:06.310
but we weren't that close.

00:42:06.310 --> 00:42:07.660
Every time she said something to me,

00:42:07.660 --> 00:42:09.670
it was just like brutally honest.

00:42:09.670 --> 00:42:12.433
Cause she had no filter.

00:42:13.490 --> 00:42:15.820
She had no filter of if she
was embarrassing anybody

00:42:15.820 --> 00:42:18.410
or embarrassing herself. And it was,

00:42:18.410 --> 00:42:21.020
I approached her with trepidation.

00:42:21.020 --> 00:42:21.853
Right?

00:42:21.853 --> 00:42:23.587
Every year at the family
reunion or at the holidays,

00:42:23.587 --> 00:42:25.960
at the New Year's Eve thing. It'd be like,

00:42:25.960 --> 00:42:28.500
she was the first person
to just come up to me,

00:42:28.500 --> 00:42:32.591
to my face and be like, you dye your hair.

00:42:35.030 --> 00:42:37.330
She was like, good Lord.

00:42:37.330 --> 00:42:39.710
Why do you? You dye your hair, don't you?

00:42:39.710 --> 00:42:42.160
Is that why you stopped?
Because of aunt Fey?

00:42:42.160 --> 00:42:44.410
It was certainly a factor. It's like, man,

00:42:44.410 --> 00:42:49.030
She's exposing my fakeness,
why am I doing this?

00:42:49.030 --> 00:42:50.830
This is the last straw.

00:42:50.830 --> 00:42:52.490
Maybe you just did it too hard.

00:42:52.490 --> 00:42:54.460
I did do it too hard.
Yeah.

00:42:54.460 --> 00:42:55.843
It crept up on me.

00:42:56.750 --> 00:42:58.170
You got too used to it.

00:42:58.170 --> 00:43:00.440
And it's not the type of
person that I wanted to be,

00:43:00.440 --> 00:43:02.060
but I didn't want to. And, also,

00:43:02.060 --> 00:43:04.670
it doesn't feel good to
be embarrassed by somebody

00:43:04.670 --> 00:43:07.150
or to be afraid that
somebody is gonna expose you.

00:43:07.150 --> 00:43:10.140
Yeah, because she had the
inability to be embarrassed,

00:43:10.140 --> 00:43:13.020
she embarrassed you, she
made it happen in you.

00:43:13.020 --> 00:43:13.853
Oh yeah.

00:43:13.853 --> 00:43:16.020
This reminds me of Jesse.

00:43:16.020 --> 00:43:20.940
My wife had an aunt who
passed away years ago.

00:43:20.940 --> 00:43:22.313
Her grandmother's sister.

00:43:25.860 --> 00:43:27.150
Oh, you talking about brain woman?

00:43:27.150 --> 00:43:30.750
Yeah. She actually had, back in the day,

00:43:30.750 --> 00:43:33.020
when lobotomies were a thing.

00:43:33.020 --> 00:43:37.190
Now, again, in actual medical history,

00:43:37.190 --> 00:43:40.590
the lobotomy was any sort
of surgery on the brain

00:43:40.590 --> 00:43:42.090
to address a condition, right?

00:43:43.110 --> 00:43:44.130
I don't remember all the things

00:43:44.130 --> 00:43:45.280
that she was struggling with,

00:43:45.280 --> 00:43:47.090
but it was very significant, right?

00:43:47.090 --> 00:43:49.080
And it was affecting her life.

00:43:49.080 --> 00:43:51.410
So they did the experimental
surgery that they did

00:43:51.410 --> 00:43:54.720
in like the fifties where
they would literally

00:43:54.720 --> 00:43:57.010
sever the connection between
the left and the right brain.

00:43:57.010 --> 00:43:59.450
This is something that's
been done and is fascinating

00:43:59.450 --> 00:44:03.140
to study the people who've
had their two lobes separated

00:44:03.140 --> 00:44:04.500
because they can literally be two

00:44:04.500 --> 00:44:05.690
different people inside.

00:44:05.690 --> 00:44:08.740
It's fascinating to read
the literature on this.

00:44:08.740 --> 00:44:11.620
But whatever they did,
when they went in there,

00:44:11.620 --> 00:44:16.310
they also got rid of any
impulse to be embarrassed

00:44:16.310 --> 00:44:19.290
or self-conscious about anything. And so,

00:44:19.290 --> 00:44:20.620
and this happened when she was a teenager.

00:44:20.620 --> 00:44:24.440
So she lived her entire
life. Very productive life,

00:44:24.440 --> 00:44:25.963
married with kids.

00:44:26.880 --> 00:44:28.000
She was a loose cannon?

00:44:28.000 --> 00:44:29.600
She was so direct.

00:44:29.600 --> 00:44:34.170
Like she was the kind of
person that you go and see her

00:44:35.060 --> 00:44:36.910
and you knock on her door
and she'd opened the door

00:44:36.910 --> 00:44:37.760
and she'd look at you and she'd be like,

00:44:37.760 --> 00:44:39.880
well, you've gained weight, you know?

00:44:39.880 --> 00:44:41.740
And some people do that as a jab

00:44:41.740 --> 00:44:42.650
and she was just doing
it because she was like,

00:44:42.650 --> 00:44:44.430
well, you probably have gained weight.

00:44:44.430 --> 00:44:45.930
And she's gonna just tell you.

00:44:45.930 --> 00:44:48.470
It the first thing that
came through her brain

00:44:48.470 --> 00:44:50.084
and out her mouth.

00:44:50.084 --> 00:44:51.130
But I mean, Jesse's
got some great stories,

00:44:51.130 --> 00:44:52.270
which I can't remember any of them

00:44:52.270 --> 00:44:54.050
for some reason at this moment.

00:44:54.050 --> 00:44:57.450
But yeah, she could not be embarrassed,

00:44:57.450 --> 00:45:00.120
but you knew that it
was based on something

00:45:00.120 --> 00:45:01.480
that had happened to her.

00:45:01.480 --> 00:45:04.100
And so there was this
forgiveness that everybody,

00:45:04.100 --> 00:45:06.100
this grace that everybody extended to her.

00:45:06.100 --> 00:45:08.300
But it's a bit much, right?

00:45:08.300 --> 00:45:11.510
So kind of, if you weren't ready for it,

00:45:11.510 --> 00:45:12.990
you had to like bolster yourself

00:45:12.990 --> 00:45:14.850
to be in her presence probably.

00:45:14.850 --> 00:45:15.683
Yeah.

00:45:16.920 --> 00:45:18.670
But it was really just a reflection

00:45:18.670 --> 00:45:22.223
on any insecurity you may have.

00:45:23.090 --> 00:45:24.130
Cause some people are like, man,

00:45:24.130 --> 00:45:25.360
I wish I could be like that.

00:45:25.360 --> 00:45:27.940
I wish we could all just be blunt

00:45:28.990 --> 00:45:30.523
and truthful about everything.

00:45:31.620 --> 00:45:32.500
Actually, I don't know.

00:45:32.500 --> 00:45:34.600
I don't know. Would we really like that?

00:45:34.600 --> 00:45:37.740
And are we even capable?
We're not capable of it.

00:45:37.740 --> 00:45:39.390
We're not capable of just getting rid of

00:45:39.390 --> 00:45:42.780
whatever it was that
generated the tendency

00:45:42.780 --> 00:45:46.290
to be embarrassed about
things. And is probably,

00:45:46.290 --> 00:45:49.811
I have to assume, that
if it's part of the code,

00:45:49.811 --> 00:45:53.260
it was and continues to
be useful to some degree.

00:45:53.260 --> 00:45:56.490
I mean the ability to be
embarrassed about things,

00:45:56.490 --> 00:45:59.550
I think is a pretty good social regulator.

00:45:59.550 --> 00:46:03.280
I guess, as if the anticipation of it.

00:46:03.280 --> 00:46:04.940
Like once you've
experienced embarrassment,

00:46:04.940 --> 00:46:07.040
you want to avoid that.

00:46:07.040 --> 00:46:09.708
It's like, okay, you don't want

00:46:09.708 --> 00:46:12.620
to show any weakness or any stupidity.

00:46:12.620 --> 00:46:16.320
It's like, damn, she can't
even walk across a string

00:46:16.320 --> 00:46:19.010
of Christmas lights without
bringing down the whole party.

00:46:19.010 --> 00:46:21.580
You know? It's like, you
don't want to show yourself

00:46:21.580 --> 00:46:24.693
as a liability in any way.

00:46:25.860 --> 00:46:26.790
Well, we talked about that.

00:46:26.790 --> 00:46:28.480
We've talked about this before, too.

00:46:28.480 --> 00:46:30.733
I don't know how we were
coming back to this,

00:46:32.102 --> 00:46:36.930
but the fact that a gorilla,
like a Silverback gorilla,

00:46:36.930 --> 00:46:39.903
in order to express dominance
over another Silverback,

00:46:41.564 --> 00:46:45.860
will get an erection in the
middle of trying to fight.

00:46:45.860 --> 00:46:47.630
You keep wanting to talk about it.

00:46:47.630 --> 00:46:48.990
No, but I think this is,

00:46:48.990 --> 00:46:53.890
because what he's saying
is I'm not scared of you.

00:46:53.890 --> 00:46:56.900
I'm not embarrassed at all.
Look what I am capable of.

00:46:56.900 --> 00:46:59.213
I'm doing right now. Look,
what's happening right now.

00:46:59.213 --> 00:47:02.000
Look at what I'm capable of right now.

00:47:02.000 --> 00:47:04.653
Right, yeah. I mean,
gorillas figured it out.

00:47:06.445 --> 00:47:08.418
I don't think that's necessarily the path.

00:47:08.418 --> 00:47:09.649
I don't think that's the objective.

00:47:09.649 --> 00:47:13.663
To walk around with your
testicles everywhere.

00:47:15.415 --> 00:47:18.020
I'm just saying that the ability,

00:47:18.020 --> 00:47:19.670
in certain circumstances, the ability

00:47:19.670 --> 00:47:22.153
to overcome your embarrassment,
there is something to it.

00:47:22.153 --> 00:47:25.080
I mean, you could play possum
or you could stand back up

00:47:25.080 --> 00:47:26.520
and you could say, you
could just own it and say,

00:47:26.520 --> 00:47:28.680
you know what? I tripped.

00:47:28.680 --> 00:47:30.650
Has anyone else here ever tripped?

00:47:30.650 --> 00:47:32.450
I bet you've all tripped before.

00:47:32.450 --> 00:47:34.600
It could have been anybody.

00:47:34.600 --> 00:47:35.550
It'd be a nice speech to have prepared.

00:47:35.550 --> 00:47:38.863
Any of you people, it could
have been you, not me.

00:47:40.569 --> 00:47:43.847
So shut your cake hole.
Stop laughing at me.

00:47:44.945 --> 00:47:47.090
That might be more embarrassing

00:47:47.090 --> 00:47:49.180
if you, I mean, that
got a little aggressive.

00:47:49.180 --> 00:47:51.270
But I do think there is a good
speech that you could have

00:47:51.270 --> 00:47:53.540
in your back pocket for
when you embarrass yourself,

00:47:53.540 --> 00:47:56.120
physically, in mixed company.

00:47:56.120 --> 00:47:59.150
People in glass houses
shouldn't throw stones

00:47:59.150 --> 00:48:00.450
or whatever the saying is.

00:48:03.350 --> 00:48:04.940
You bunch of hypocrites!

00:48:04.940 --> 00:48:06.300
